{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfhyrK5c7asuMQ345dQoZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate for round 4, slot 0 (exploratory), and it passes the self-check. The check ran a short smoke fit (1 chain, 100 draws) and printed `OK`, with ELPD-LOO −1002.9. The best model, `length_normalised_chance_vs_motif`, is at −995.1 from its full fit, so the two numbers aren't directly comparable.\n\n**Model:** `hot_hand_only_suspicion`\n\n**Hypothesis:** People judge a sequence random to the extent a plain fair coin explains it better than the only two kinds of \"non-random\" coin they ever suspect:\n- a streaky coin that tends to repeat its last outcome by an unknown amount;\n- a coin biased towards heads or tails by an unknown amount.\n\nThe unknown amounts are averaged out, and the evidence is weighed per flip. Switching is never suspicious in itself, so perfect alternation and repeating patterns (period 3 or 4) count as maximally random. That is where it disagrees most with the best model, which condemns them as repeating motifs. This aims at the critique's strongest finding: the best model over-penalises perfect alternation, people pick it 0.768 of the time against a predicted 0.827, and this survives the false-discovery correction. Among sequences with similar switch counts, the new model still penalises streaks and heads/tails imbalance.\n\n**Implementation:**\n- Each sequence's evidence is computed in closed form, once per distinct sequence.\n- There are three shared parameters: how streaky the suspected coin is imagined to be, the per-flip length weighting, and the population scale of sensitivity.\n- Each person has their own decision sensitivity (non-centred, 400 slots), so the next design can predict new participants.\n- It needs no loops or scans, so it should fit well within the 30-minute admission limit.\n\n**Why this one:** I first compared options with a quick fit that gives everyone the same sensitivity. This model scored 1221.5 against about 1213.6 for the best model's mechanism, with one fewer parameter. Models built on run lengths alone, take-the-best cue order, how varied the short patterns are, and higher-order context were all clearly worse (1245–1328).\n\n**Still open:**\n- **PSIS-LOO reliability:** the smoke fit flagged it as unreliable, with 8.8% of trials having a high Pareto k against a 1% tolerance. That may be the 100-draw fit, but if it persists in the full fit, this model's ELPD-LOO (the score used to rank and prune models) won't be trusted.\n- **Not checked:** the self-check doesn't test convergence on the full multi-chain fit or novelty against the other models.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_0/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3493,"cache_read_input_tokens":79477,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3493,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3e0c4a5f-e236-4b05-a604-4b4d69fb46d1","uuid":"4dcb19ff-751c-47b6-bbd6-0c744399b2d8","timestamp":"2026-10-04T22:24:53.258Z","request_id":"req_011CfhyrJfYYKnLxRvqQaFy1"}
